lood’s role in preventing bleeding. This ties platelet biology to the practical outcome of hemostasis. [Data: Entities (3031, 2902); Relationships (5958)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Electrolytes bridge circulation to cellular exchange and are tied to nervous system function\",\n      \"explanation\": \"ELECTROLYTES are described as charged ions in the blood that regulate movement of nutrients into cells and removal of waste out of cells, and they are commonly monitored in routine blood chemistry to assess body balance and function. This positions electrolytes as mediators between what the blood carries (nutrients/waste) and what cells take in or expel. [Data: Entities (1757, 904, 905)]\\n\\nA specific relationship links ELECTROLYTES to NERVES, stating electrolytes help nerves function properly. This adds a concrete physiological dependency (nerve function) to the broader concept of electrolyte balance. [Data: Entities (1757); Relationships (2924)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Nutrients and nutrient absorption connect digestive intake to blood-based distribution\",\n      \"explanation\": \"NUTRIENTS are defined as food components that are digested and absorbed—primarily in the small intestine—into the bloodstream, after which the blood transports them throughout the body to support metabolism and cellular/organ function. The network therefore ties nutrient intake to systemic distribution via blood. [Data: Entities (904, 337)]\\n\\nNUTRIENT ABSORPTION is explicitly linked to NUTRIENTS as the process by which nutrients are taken up from food during digestion. In addition, GENES are described as providing cellular instructions that can include absorbing nutrients, linking the concept of nutrient absorption to genetic control of cellular function. [Data: Entities (1079, 904); Relationships (1674, 1656)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Waste handling is split between blood transport and elimination pathways, including digestive waste removal\",\n      \"explanation\": \"WASTE is described as unwanted/unneeded material from body processes or digestion; BLOOD plays a transport role by carrying waste away from tissues and cells toward organs that remove them. This directly aligns with the BLOOD → CARRYING AWAY WASTE relationship in the network. [Data: Entities (905, 337, 3046); Relationships (5982)]\\n\\nThe community also includes a digestive-system framing of elimination: WASTE is connected to WASTE REMOVAL as the digestive process responsible for eliminating waste left over from digestion. This establishes that waste is handled both via circulation (transport) and via digestive elimination pathways. [Data: Entities (905); Relationships (1771)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Observable bleeding cues and staging nuance: toilet paper as a bleeding indicator and stage-4 cancer not always blood-borne spread\",\n      \"explanation\": \"TOILET PAPER appears as a practical indicator in a medical context: blood may be noticed on toilet paper with rectal bleeding, and it may also show blood after a cystoscopy if urethral bleeding occurs, sometimes alongside pink-tinged urine. A relationship specifically ties TOILET PAPER to BLEEDING FROM THE URETHRA post-cystoscopy. [Data: Entities (5804); Relationships (13164)]\\n\\nSeparately, STAGE 4 CANCER is linked to BLOOD-BORNE SPREAD with an explicit clarification that not all stage 4 cancers have spread through the blood (i.e., stage 4 does not necessarily imply blood-borne metastatic spread). This adds nuance to how “advanced disease” relates (or does not always relate) to hematogenous dissemination. [Data: Entities (4581, 4610); Relationships (9835)]\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"rating\": 3.4,\n  \"rating_explanation\": \"The community’s impact severity is low-to-moderate because it primarily describes general physiology and clinical diagnostic concepts (blood composition, transport, lab testing, and bleeding indicators) without indicating coordinated harmful activity.